Expected elements: present

All MUST-SHOW items are present and readable in this theme: nav and resolver, breadcrumb, hash with outcome badge, the `Not observable` badge with the published pruning reason and the permanence sentence, the affirmative provenance marker as the Overview grid's first `Data` row (badge plus the capture's own sentences — a row, not a band, and the only marker on the page), all sections with permanent-tense empty copy, and a footer whose heart, CodeTracer and GitHub marks are all drawn and clearly visible against the surface. No error colour and no danger tone anywhere, which is the right call and the hardest one.

The theme reads as designed rather than inverted: a warm neutral canvas, white cards above it, one restrained green accent family, near-black body type well above the contrast floor.

Two colour-role problems. Green carries three unrelated meanings on one screen — transaction outcome (`Succeeded`), finality phase (`Finalized`) and data provenance (`Real Aztec mainnet data`) — so provenance, which is a category of data rather than a status, scans as a third "everything is fine" chip. And the `Not observable` badge is toned exactly like the `Yes` canonicality chip: the page's defining state gets the same colour weight as a routine boolean while three green chips take the eye first. Colour therefore ranks this page's information backwards, even though every individual choice is defensible.

Fix first: give provenance a tone outside the status family, and let `Not observable` carry more neutral weight than `Yes`.

Rating: 7/10

